\section*{Robust Noise Floor Estimation from FFT Bin Powers}

Given FFT bin powers \( P_k \sim \text{Exp}(\mu) \), the goal is to estimate the mean noise power \( \mu \) in the presence of signal contamination, without temporal averaging.

\subsection*{1. Quantile-Based Estimator}

Let \( p_q \) be the \( q \)-th quantile of the bin powers. Then the unbiased estimate is:

\[
\mu = \frac{p_q}{- \ln(1 - q)}
\]

\subsection*{2. Average of Lowest Fraction \( q \)}

Let \( \bar{P}_{\text{lowest } q} \) be the average of the lowest \( q \cdot N \) sorted bin powers:

\[
\mu = \bar{P}_{\text{lowest } q} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - q + q \ln(1 - q)}
\]

\subsection*{3. Average of Bins Below a Threshold}

Let \( p_q \) be the \( q \)-th quantile, and define a threshold \( T \cdot p_q \). Let \( z = T \cdot (-\ln(1 - q)) \). Then the expected mean of bins below this threshold is:

\[
\mu = \bar{P}_{\text{subset}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 - \frac{z e^{-z}}{1 - e^{-z}}}
\]

\subsection*{FFT Power Normalization}

For an FFT of length \( N \) with window \( w[n] \), the bin power should be normalized as:

\[
P_k = \frac{|X_k|^2}{N \cdot \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} w[n]^2}
\]

\subsection*{Notes}
\begin{itemize}
  \item All quantile and average computations must be done in the \textbf{linear power domain}, not dB.
  \item These formulas assume bins are independent or that enough are unaffected by signal.
  \item For rectangular windows and noise-only input, quantile-based estimates remain valid.
\end{itemize}
